Key Components of EC2 Auto Scaling

1. Launch Template (or Launch Configuration)
Defines the configuration for EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling Group.

Includes:
          AMI (Amazon Machine Image)
          Instance type (e.g., t2.micro)
          Key pair
          Security groups
          IAM roles
          User data (optional boot scripts)

 Launch Templates are recommended over Launch Configurations (which are legacy).

2. Auto Scaling Group (ASG)
Core component that manages a group of EC2 instances.
        Defines:
                    Minimum, Maximum, and Desired number of instances
                    Subnets and Availability Zones
                    Scaling policies
                    Health checks and replacement of unhealthy instances
                    Automatically adds/removes instances based on demand and policies.

3. Scaling Policies
Used to control when and how to scale the instances.
Types:
        Target Tracking Scaling (Recommended): Example: Maintain CPU at 50%
        
        Step Scaling: Define thresholds and step sizes (e.g., add 1 instance if CPU > 70%)
        
        Simple Scaling (Basic, older method):  Perform one action based on one condition

4. Health Checks
Ensures only healthy instances stay running.
        Types:
        EC2 health check
        ELB health check (if Load Balancer is used)
        Unhealthy instances are terminated and replaced automatically.

5. Load Balancer (Optional but Important)
Distributes traffic evenly across EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling Group.

Types:
      Application Load Balancer (ALB)
      Network Load Balancer (NLB)
      Helps in high availability and fault tolerance.

6. Amazon CloudWatch
      Monitors metrics (CPU, memory, etc.)
      Triggers scaling actions based on alarms
      Can also monitor custom metrics

7. Notifications (Optional)
Uses Amazon SNS (Simple Notification Service) to send alerts about scaling events (e.g., instance launched/terminated)



Maintain zero downtime (if setup properly)

Reduce manual intervention

Ensure smooth scaling and failover
